\subsection{Specify Copy-Back on Success for an Out Parameter}

Native libraries often return a status but write a result through
a separate pointer. Describe what the guest observes if those writes
are partial. In this example a synchronous C function fills a byte
buffer and returns a status plus a logical count. The binding
commits exactly that prefix on success and no bytes on failure.
Assume the library respects the passed capacity, does not retain
the pointer, and has no other observable effects in this binding.
Use zero-initialized native scratch under a fixed capacity limit and
hold the guest destination through a local root. Assume no guest
reentry or concurrent mutation of that destination and use the
VM's array-copy API when committing.

\begin{sudocode}[Commit a checked native result]
\State $cap\gets$ checked, bounded length of destination
\State $h\gets$ root of managed destination
\State $buf\gets$ zeroed native storage of capacity $cap$
\State arrange cleanup of $buf$ and $h$ on every exit
\State $(status,n)\gets\Call{ForeignFill}{buf,cap}$
  \Comment{No raw managed pointer crosses the call}
\If{$status$ indicates failure}
  \State \Return translated error
\EndIf
\If{$n<0$ or $n>cap$}
  \State \Return binding error \Comment{Do not copy partial output}
\EndIf
\State copy $buf[0:n]$ into destination resolved from $h$
\State \Return $n$
\end{sudocode}

Start the guest array at $(9,9,9,9)$. Have the native fixture
write 2 and 3 and return success with count 2. The guest gets count
2 and array $(2,3,9,9)$. Force a moving collection while the thread
is outside managed code; the result must be copied into the moved
array. When the fixture instead returns failure after its first write,
the guest sees an error and the original contents. A successful status
with count 5 is also an error and must leave all four cells untouched.

In \Cref{fig:visual-250}, the native writes appear only in scratch until the success status and count have both passed validation.

