The invariant is that native writes are unobservable in the guest
until checked commit, and only the declared prefix changes at commit.
This does not sandbox arbitrary C code: checking the reported count
cannot undo an actual write past the scratch capacity. The copy and
initialization costs belong in benchmarks against a pinning design.

\section{Keep the public convention stable}

Do not unwind guest exceptions across arbitrary host frames; translate them into a status and, when appropriate, an error handle with a documented lifetime. Decide whether host code may reenter the same instance and which threads may attach. Document that policy along with the signature. Chapter~42 of the Modern Compiler Dossier, \emph{Adaptive ABI Negotiation}, explores changing private calling conventions between cooperating units. A published foreign entry point instead needs a stable adapter, including across changes to tagging, collector strategy, or internal register use.
